.TH ANKER\-LAUNCHER 1 "2026-11-18" "anker-launcher" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
anker\-launcher \- run the AnkerGames launcher with GPU rendering and instant scrolling
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B anker\-launcher
[\fIURL\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B anker\-launcher
is a wrapper around the upstream AnkerGames desktop client installed at
.IR /opt/anker\-launcher\-bin/AppRun .
It works around two separate upstream problems; no file under
.B /opt
is modified.
.SS Software rendering by default
The client is a Tauri application using
.B WebKitGTK
4.1. On startup it sets two WebKit environment variables, but only when they
are not already present:
.IP \(bu 2
.B WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1
.IP \(bu 2
.B WEBKIT_SKIA_GPU_PAINTING_THREADS=0
.P
With those values WebKit rasterises page content on the CPU and the GTK UI
process presents every frame through a freshly allocated
.B wl_shm
pool, so the cost of a frame is proportional to window area. At 1280x800 the
client kept two processes busy repainting about 60 times a second even while
idle, and at 2560x1440 scrolling became unusable.
.P
This wrapper presets GPU values instead. Measured on an RX 9070 with Mesa 26,
scrolling CPU in the UI process fell from roughly 14% to 3%.
.SS Scroll animation pacing
WebKitGTK eases wheel scrolling inside the web process on a fixed 60 Hz clock.
On a high refresh display that makes scrolling feel like a 60 Hz panel and lag
behind the pointer, even after the rendering fix above. WebKitGTK exposes no
environment variable for the setting, so this wrapper compiles a small
.B LD_PRELOAD
shim (source embedded in the script, cached under
.IR ~/.cache/anker\-launcher )
that hooks
.BR webkit_web_view_get_settings ()
and calls
.BR webkit_settings_set_enable_smooth_scrolling ( settings ", 0)."
Scrolling then applies each wheel event immediately instead of animating it.
.P
The shim removes its own
.B LD_PRELOAD
entry once loaded in the launcher, so WebKit helper processes and the Wine or
Proton processes that later start a game do not inherit it. A compiler is
needed only the first time; without
.BR gcc (1)
the wrapper starts the client untweaked instead of failing.
.P
Any arguments are passed through to the AppImage unchanged, so URI activation
from the desktop entry
.RB ( x\-scheme\-handler=ankergames )
keeps working.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ANKER_SOFTWARE_RENDER
Set to
.B 1
to skip the rendering fix and launch the client exactly as upstream ships it,
i.e. with software rendering. Use this if a driver update makes the GPU path
render a blank or corrupt window.
.TP
.B ANKER_NO_SCROLL_TWEAK
Set to
.B 1
to skip the scroll shim and keep WebKit's animated (60 Hz) scrolling.
.TP
.B WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER
Preset by the wrapper to
.B 0
(dmabuf buffers shared with the compositor). A value supplied in the calling
environment is preserved.
.TP
.B WEBKIT_SKIA_GPU_PAINTING_THREADS
Preset by the wrapper to
.B 4
(GPU painting threads). A value supplied in the calling environment is
preserved. Setting it to
.B 0
reproduces the slow software path even when the dmabuf override is active.
.TP
.B LD_PRELOAD
Prepended with the compiled scroll shim. Existing entries are kept and are
still visible to the launcher's own children.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the launcher normally:
.IP
.EX
anker\-launcher
.EE
.P
Open the launcher on a store page:
.IP
.EX
anker\-launcher "ankergames://store"
.EE
.P
Compare against upstream behaviour, or work around a broken GPU path:
.IP
.EX
ANKER_SOFTWARE_RENDER=1 anker\-launcher
.EE
.P
Keep the rendering fix but restore WebKit's animated scrolling:
.IP
.EX
ANKER_NO_SCROLL_TWEAK=1 anker\-launcher
.EE
.P
Use a different number of GPU painting threads:
.IP
.EX
WEBKIT_SKIA_GPU_PAINTING_THREADS=2 anker\-launcher
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The launcher exited normally.
.TP
.B other
Whatever the AppImage returned; the wrapper does not alter exit codes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/anker\-launcher\-bin/AppRun
The upstream launcher that this script execs.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/anker\-launcher
Distribution launcher entry point, shadowed for interactive use by this script
when
.I ~/.local/bin
comes first in
.BR PATH .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/applications/anker\-launcher.desktop
Desktop entry that points at this wrapper, so menu and
.B ankergames://
launches get the fixes.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/anker\-launcher/scrollhook\-HASH.so
Compiled scroll shim, keyed by a hash of the embedded source. Stale builds from
older revisions are deleted automatically.
.TP
.I ~/.config/net.ankergames.launcher/
Launcher preferences, library and session data. Setting
.B presentation.reduce_motion
to
.B true
there stops the store UI's constant animation loop, which otherwise repaints at
the frame-rate ceiling even when idle.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dotfiles\-sync (1),
.BR gcc (1),
.BR webkit2gtk\-4\.1 (3)
.SH BUGS
Both fixes depend on upstream behaviour that a future release could change:
the client must keep filling in the two WebKit variables only when they are
unset, and it must keep fetching its settings object through
.BR webkit_web_view_get_settings ().
If a new version assigns those variables unconditionally or caches settings
internally elsewhere, the corresponding workaround silently stops applying.
.SH AUTHOR
Written for local use.
